{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>The enemy said: I will pursue, etc.</b> Pharaoh schemed to do to Israel along the lines of what is said (Job 12:23), ״He makes nations great and destroys them,״ and as it is written (Psalms 92:7–8), ״A brutish man does not know, and a fool does not understand this — when the wicked sprout like grass and all evildoers flourish, it is for their destruction forever.״ For it is the way of the world: when one wishes to cast another to the ground, the higher he first raises him, the greater his fall. For one who falls from a high place is not like one who falls from a low place, as it is written (Lamentations 2:1), ״He cast down from heaven to earth the splendor of Israel״ — from the highest roof to the deepest pit. As a parable of a king against whom a man from a poor and despised village rebelled. What did the king do? He enriched him and exalted him until he grew very great, and his servants marveled at this. And after he stood at his greatness for several years, he took vengeance upon him and killed him together with his entire household, and took all that was his, and left him no survivor or remnant. For then the vengeance was more openly manifest and publicized than if he had killed him while he was still a lowly man of no account." "So Pharaoh said: what vengeance is this, that I should avenge myself against slaves who were occupied with mortar and bricks, when Israel had become utterly impoverished? Rather, on the contrary — I will divide spoils for them from my own property and from the property of my people, by having them borrow vessels of silver and gold and garments, and through this they will be well-supplied and esteemed. And this is what it says: ״The enemy said: I will pursue, I will overtake them״ — when the day comes, but not now, immediately; rather, first ״I will divide spoils״ from my own. And when ״my soul will be filled with them״ — that is, when my soul is filled with all that good and he is quiet and tranquil in his palace — then ״I will draw my sword, my hand will dispossess them,״ so that the vengeance I will wreak upon them for rebelling against me and bringing ten plagues upon Egypt will be open and publicized. And according to this, the language ״תִּמְלָאֵמוֹ תּוֹרִישֵׁמוֹ״ is precisely construed, for it all refers to Israel. But what Rashi explained, that ״תִּמְלָאֵמוֹ״ means 'my soul will be filled from them' — that is far from the precise construal of the language. And according to our approach, what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Gittin 56b) is also explained: ״Whoever oppresses Israel becomes a head,״ and that is in order to magnify his downfall afterward. And in this manner, it seems to me, is also what is written once as ״יָרָה בַיָּם״ and once as ״רָמָה בַיָּם״ — and Rashi explained that they first went up and afterward went down, and this accords with the path of allusion for all these words of ours. And so Rashi cites on the verse ״מַשְׂגִּיא לַגּוֹיִם וַיְאַבְּדֵם״ (Job 12:23) on the verse ״לִפְנֵי בַּעַל צְפֹן״ (Exodus 14:2), and this is a precious derashah." "And what he wrote, ״I will draw my sword,״ means to say: even if there were no sword in my hand, nevertheless ״my hand will dispossess them,״ for by my hand alone, without a sword, I will dispossess them, for they are weak. And some say that ״תִּמְלָאֵמוֹ״ is ״תּוֹרִישֵׁמוֹ״ — his own mouth caused him to say thus, for in the end he would dispossess to them all his possessions in the spoils of the sea, and fill Israel with his spoils."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:30.179550053Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}